The Security Council,

Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling its resolution 2202 (2015) of 17 February 2015, and the letter dated 13 April 2014 from the Permanent Representative of the Russian Federation to the United Nations addressed to the President of the Security Council (S/2014/264),

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation in Ukraine, in particular the increase in civilian casualties, including women and children, and the destruction of civilian infrastructure,

Emphasizing the need for all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the Geneva Conventions of 1949 and the Additional Protocols thereto of 1977,

Stressing the need for safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all affected areas in Ukraine, including the delivery of humanitarian assistance and the evacuation of civilians,

Emphasizing the need for a peaceful settlement of the conflict through political dialogue and negotiations,

Noting the efforts of the Secretary-General and other international actors to address the humanitarian situation,

1. Demands an immediate cessation of hostilities by all parties to the conflict;

2. Demands that all parties ensure safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all affected areas in Ukraine, including by establishing humanitarian corridors and allowing the delivery of humanitarian assistance and the evacuation of civilians;

3. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including by protecting civilians and civilian infrastructure, and to refrain from attacks on medical facilities and personnel;

4. Urges all parties to engage in political dialogue and negotiations aimed at a peaceful settlement of the conflict;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution within 30 days;

6. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

The Security Council,

Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Recalling its resolution 2202 (2015) of 17 February 2015, and the letter dated 13 April 2014 from the Permanent Representative of the Russian Federation to the United Nations addressed to the President of the Security Council (S/2014/264),

Reaffirming the need for full respect for the sovereignty, independence and territorial integrity of all States, in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations,

Expressing grave concern at the deteriorating humanitarian situation in Ukraine, in particular the increase in civilian casualties, including women and children, and the destruction of civilian infrastructure,

Emphasizing the need for all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including the Geneva Conventions of 1949 and the Additional Protocols thereto of 1977,

Stressing the need for safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all affected areas in Ukraine, including the delivery of humanitarian assistance and the evacuation of civilians,

Guided by the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence,

Emphasizing the need for a peaceful settlement of the conflict through political dialogue and negotiations,

Noting the efforts of the Secretary-General and other international actors to address the humanitarian situation,

1. Demands an immediate cessation of hostilities by all parties to the conflict;

2. Demands that all parties ensure safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all affected areas in Ukraine, in accordance with international humanitarian law and the humanitarian principles of humanity, neutrality, impartiality and independence, in coordination with relevant international humanitarian actors, including by establishing humanitarian corridors and allowing the delivery of humanitarian assistance and the voluntary and safe evacuation of civilians;

3. Calls upon all parties to respect international humanitarian law, including by protecting civilians and civilian infrastructure, including medical facilities, schools and critical civilian infrastructure, and to refrain from attacks on medical facilities and personnel;

4. Urges all parties to engage in direct political dialogue and negotiations in good faith, with a view to immediate de-escalation and a peaceful settlement of the conflict;

5. Requests the Secretary-General to report to the Security Council on the implementation of this resolution within 30 days;

6. Decides to remain seized of the matter.

